/-! # Local equations of prime divisors on regular schemes

Source: Stacks Project, divisors.tex, Tags 0AGA, 0AG9 and 0AGB
(blueprint: lem-per-regular-prime-divisor-local-equations).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- An integral codimension-one closed subscheme of an integral regular locally Noetherian
scheme has an effective Cartier ideal. Every stalk generator is prime, its image is a
uniformizer at the generic point, and localization at its principal prime ideal identifies
with the generic stalk through the canonical generization homomorphism.

Source: Stacks Project, divisors.tex, Tags 0AGA (lemma-weil-divisor-is-cartier-UFD),
0AG9 (lemma-integral-effective-Cartier-divisor-dvr), and the second proof paragraph of
0AGB (lemma-codim-1-part).
(blueprint: lem-per-regular-prime-divisor-local-equations).

Proof:
1. On a Noetherian affine neighbourhood Spec R of x, write D as Spec (R/J). Integrality
   makes J prime and identifies its point with the generic point of D. The affine stalk
   and tilde stalk localization results identify A with R_q, the ideal stalk with J R_q,
   and the generic stalk with R_J [lem-weil-pbc-affine-open-stalk-localization,
   lem-weil-pbc-spec-tilde-stalk-localization].
2. The fraction maps (a/s)/(b/t) to at/(sb) and a/b to (a/1)/(b/1) identify
   A_(J R_q) with R_J. They agree with the germs on R and hence with generization.
3. Prime extension and contraction preserve strict inclusions, so dimension one of this
   localization says that J R_q is a nonzero height-one prime.
4. A regular parameter system, with the empty selected sublist, makes A a UFD
   [lem-trf-regular-local-ufd-boundary-factorization]. Its height-one prime is principal
   [lem-trf-ufd-height-one]. Every generator is nonzero and prime since the ideal is
   nonzero, proper and prime.
5. Write its generator as a/s with a in J. Choose finitely many generators a_i of J,
   and clear their finitely many equality denominators in the equations a_i = b_i a/t_i
   in R_q. After inverting their product, J is generated by the nonzero element a,
   which is a nonzerodivisor in this domain.
6. These neighbourhoods, together with affine opens outside D where the ideal is (1),
   give the effective Cartier condition [def-nagata-effective-cartier-ideal].
7. For any generator f, its principal ideal is the same prime J R_q. Step 2 gives the
   asserted canonical localization at (f), whose maximal ideal is generated by f.
8. The generic stalk is regular local of dimension one, hence a DVR
   [lem-sc-regular-dim1-dvr]. The maximal-ideal equality in step 7 says exactly that
   the image of f is a uniformizer, including when x is the generic point.
-/
theorem regularPrimeDivisorLocalEquations
    {X D : Scheme.{u}} [IsIntegral X] [IsIntegral D]
    (hX : IsRegularScheme X) (i : D ⟶ X) [IsClosedImmersion i]
    (ξ : D) (hξ : IsGenericPoint ξ Set.univ)
    (hdim : ringKrullDim (X.presheaf.stalk (i ξ)) = 1) :
    i.ker.IsInvertible ∧ IsDiscreteValuationRing (X.presheaf.stalk (i ξ)) ∧
      ∀ (x : D) (f : X.presheaf.stalk (i x)),
        Ideal.span {f} = RingHom.ker (i.stalkMap x).hom →
          Prime f ∧
          (∃ hp : (Ideal.span {f}).IsPrime,
            ∃ e : @Localization.AtPrime (X.presheaf.stalk (i x)) _ (Ideal.span {f}) hp ≃+*
                X.presheaf.stalk (i ξ),
              ∀ a : X.presheaf.stalk (i x),
                e (algebraMap (X.presheaf.stalk (i x))
                  (@Localization.AtPrime (X.presheaf.stalk (i x)) _ (Ideal.span {f}) hp)
                  a) =
                    X.presheaf.stalkSpecializes
                      ((hξ.specializes (Set.mem_univ x)).map i.continuous) a) ∧
          Ideal.span {X.presheaf.stalkSpecializes
            ((hξ.specializes (Set.mem_univ x)).map i.continuous) f} =
              IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal (X.presheaf.stalk (i ξ)) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
